"Shen/JIT port definitions, lowered at stage 0 with the kernel. A defun here
with the name of a kernel defun replaces it: stage 0 omits the kernel's."

"An expression runs in a call, not a tail call, so that this frame stays on
the stack: backtraces stop at it. Anything but a list evaluates to itself,
which the lowerer would make a constant: load evaluates what each define
returns, (fn F)'s printer, and compiling that cost as much as the
definition."

(defun shen-jit.eval-kl (X)
  (if (cons? X)
      (if (shen-jit.builder?)
          (let Lowered (shen-jit.lower.toplevel X ())
            (if (= (hd Lowered) defun)
                (shen-jit.install Lowered)
                (let Result ((shen-jit.install Lowered)) Result)))
          (shen-jit.not-builder eval-kl))
      X))

"Only the builder evaluates and defines (docs/design.md, The builder and isolates). An isolate
sets shen-jit.*isolate* as it starts (shen-jit.start-isolate), so eval and
eval-kl refuse there at once, before they would reach the builder's globals,
which it has not got; the runtime refuses to install anything there too."

(defun shen-jit.builder? () (trap-error (not (value shen-jit.*isolate*)) (lambda E true)))

(defun shen-jit.not-builder (F)
  (simple-error (cn (str F) ": only the builder can evaluate or define code")))

"The kernel's eval, refusing in an isolate."

(defun eval (X)
  (if (shen-jit.builder?)
      (eval-kl (shen.shen->kl (shen.process-applications (macroexpand X) (shen.find-types X))))
      (shen-jit.not-builder eval)))

"The kernel calls input+ but leaves its definition to the port (41.3)."

(defun input+ (Type Stream) (shen.input-h+ Type Stream))

"Overrides."

(defun hash (X Bound) (shen-jit.hash X Bound))

"At end of input the REPL ends the process. The kernel's REPL would report an
empty stream at every prompt, forever. Only the REPL's own read exits; a read
in user code still raises the error."

(defun shen.read-evaluate-print ()
  (do (shen-jit.mark-toplevel)
  (do (set shen-jit.*load-depth* 0)
  (do (set shen-jit.*input-mark* (shen-jit.isolate 14 () ()))
  (let Package (value shen.*package*)
    (let Lineread (shen.package-user-input Package (shen-jit.repl-read (stinput)))
      (let History (shen.update-history)
        (shen.evaluate-lineread Lineread History (value shen.*tc*)))))))))

"Each input marks how many isolates had been spawned before it, and a Ctrl-C
that stops it kills those it spawned (docs/guide.md, Isolates), so that none
is left waiting on a REPL that has gone on.

Backtraces. The REPL's read-evaluate-print runs
inside the kernel's top-level trap-error, so it marks that handler: an error
raised while it is the innermost handler, or while none is, records the
frames on the stack, and the display prints them after the message. Errors
that the program catches record nothing."

(defun shen.toplevel-display-exception (E)
  (let Message (error-to-string E)
    (do (if (= Message "interrupted") (shen-jit.isolate 14 (value shen-jit.*input-mark*) ()) skip)
    (do (pr Message (stoutput))
        (pr (shen-jit.trace-text Message) (stoutput))))))

(defun shen-jit.repl-read (Stream)
  (let Byte (shen.my-read-byte Stream)
    (if (= Byte -1)
        (do (nl 1) (shen-jit.exit 0))
        (shen.read-loop Stream Byte () (lambda X (shen.return? X))))))

"The launcher exits with status 1 after reporting an error or an unknown
argument, as Shen/Scheme does; the kernel's main only reports it."

(defun shen.x.launcher.main (Argv)
  (let Result (shen.x.launcher.launch-shen Argv)
    (let Shown (shen.x.launcher.default-handle-result Result)
      (if (and (cons? Result) (element? (hd Result) (cons error (cons unknown-arguments ()))))
          (shen-jit.exit 1)
          Shown))))

"Arities are program, so they are kept beside function cells, outside the
heap and the property vector, which an isolate starts without
(docs/design.md, The builder and isolates). These are the kernel's, with that table for the
property."

(defun arity (F) (shen-jit.arity F))

(defun shen.initialise-arity-table (L)
  (cond ((= () L) ())
        ((and (cons? L) (cons? (tl L)))
         (do (shen-jit.set-arity (hd L) (hd (tl L))) (shen.initialise-arity-table (tl (tl L)))))
        (true (simple-error "implementation error in shen.initialise-arity-table"))))

(defun shen.execute-store-arity (F N)
  (if (= 0 N)
      (shen-jit.set-arity F 0)
      (do (shen-jit.set-arity F N) (shen.update-lambdatable F N))))

"A lambda form left from before would be stale: fn makes its closure from the
arity, so the form goes, as the kernel's takes it away for a name of arity 0
or none."

(defun update-lambda-table (F N)
  (do (shen-jit.set-arity F N) (do (unput F shen.lambda-form (value *property-vector*)) F)))

(defun shen.update-lambda-table/precompiled (F N Lambda Precompiled)
  (if Precompiled
      (do (shen-jit.set-arity F N) (do (thaw Lambda) (update-lambda-table F N)))
      (update-lambda-table F N)))

"fn makes its curried closure the first time it is asked for a name, and keeps
it as the name's lambda form. The builder compiles one that calls the name, as
the kernel's fn does when a definition is read; an isolate, which cannot
evaluate, makes one from a template (shen-jit.template), which needs only the
arity. A name with no arity but a lambda form keeps that form."

(defun fn (F)
  (let Arity (shen-jit.arity F)
    (if (= Arity 0)
        (F)
        (let Form (trap-error (get F shen.lambda-form (value *property-vector*)) (lambda E ()))
          (if (= Form ())
              (if (> Arity 0)
                  (shen-jit.make-form F Arity)
                  (simple-error (cn "fn: " (shen.app F " is undefined
" shen.a))))
              Form)))))

(defun shen-jit.make-form (F N)
  (let Form (if (shen-jit.builder?) (eval-kl (shen-jit.curried (cons F ()) N)) (shen-jit.template F N))
    (do (put F shen.lambda-form Form (value *property-vector*)) Form)))

"A template's last call goes through the name's function cell, which
shen-jit.function reads inline, so a redefinition is seen, as with a lambda
form's named call. Up to four arguments the call is direct; past that the
arguments are listed and shen-jit.apply makes it. The templates of two to four
are defined as the image is built (shen-jit.initialise), lowered then, so that
they take all their arguments at once too, which stage 0 code cannot."

(defun shen-jit.template (F N)
  (cond ((= N 1) (lambda A ((shen-jit.function F) A)))
        ((= N 2) (shen-jit.template-2 F))
        ((= N 3) (shen-jit.template-3 F))
        ((= N 4) (shen-jit.template-4 F))
        (true (shen-jit.template-list F N ()))))

(defun shen-jit.template-defun (Name Vars)
  (cons defun (cons Name (cons (cons f ()) (cons (shen-jit.template-body Vars Vars) ())))))

(defun shen-jit.template-body (All Rest)
  (if (= Rest ())
      (cons (cons shen-jit.function (cons f ())) All)
      (cons lambda (cons (hd Rest) (cons (shen-jit.template-body All (tl Rest)) ())))))

(defun shen-jit.template-list (F N Args)
  (lambda X
    (if (= N 1)
        (shen-jit.apply F (reverse (cons X Args)))
        (shen-jit.template-list F (- N 1) (cons X Args)))))

"Reading a definition stores F's arity and its lambda form, which the kernel
makes by evaluating a curried lambda: one compilation per definition, about a
fifth of loading a file of definitions. fn makes the form the first time it is
asked, so a definition only takes away any form made for its old arity. The
kernel's shen.lambda-function calls gensym once per argument, so the counter
still moves by N, and later gensyms are the same. A form made later names its
parameters after F, as F/1 and so on: a fixed name would be captured by a
function of that name."

(defun shen.update-lambdatable (F N)
  (do (set shen.*gensym* (+ (value shen.*gensym*) N))
      (do (unput F shen.lambda-form (value *property-vector*)) N)))

(defun shen-jit.curried (Call N)
  (if (= N 0)
      Call
      (let X (intern (cn (str (hd Call)) (cn "/" (str N))))
        (cons lambda (cons X (cons (shen-jit.curried (append Call (cons X ())) (- N 1)) ()))))))

"Native dictionaries (src/runtime/dict.c). A dictionary is an absvector
[shen.dictionary Count Buckets], with the kernel's tag at index 0 so that its
dict? and printer still apply. The buckets start at 8 whatever size is asked
for, since a large request such as the property vector's 20000 would leave
mostly empty buckets for every collection to copy; they double when the count
exceeds them, and entries change in place. A fold works on a snapshot of the entries taken
when it starts, in no particular order, so changes made by the folded
function do not affect it. A missing key builds no message when the innermost
handler ignores it (shen-jit.quiet?)."

(defun shen.dict (N)
  (if (< N 1)
      (simple-error (cn "invalid initial dict size: " (shen.app N "" shen.s)))
      (let D (absvector 3)
        (do (address-> D 0 shen.dictionary)
            (do (address-> D 1 0)
                (address-> D 2 (shen-jit.filled-vector 8 ())))))))

(defun shen.dict-count (D) (<-address D 1))

(defun shen.<-dict (D K)
  (let Entry (shen-jit.dict-find D K)
    (if (cons? Entry)
        (tl Entry)
        (simple-error (if (shen-jit.quiet?) "" (cn "value " (shen.app K " not found in dict
" shen.a)))))))

(defun shen.dict-> (D K V) (shen-jit.dict-put D K V))

(defun shen.dict-rm (D K) (shen-jit.dict-remove D K))

(defun shen.dict-fold (F D Acc) (shen-jit.fold-entries F (shen-jit.dict-entries D) Acc))

(defun shen-jit.fold-entries (F Entries Acc)
  (if (= () Entries)
      Acc
      (shen-jit.fold-entries F (tl Entries) (((F (hd (hd Entries))) (tl (hd Entries))) Acc))))

"tuple? tests a tag at index 0 in one inline primitive; the kernel's needs a
trap-error in case the absvector is empty."

(defun tuple? (X) (shen-jit.tagged? X shen.tuple))

"A Prolog variable is an immediate value holding the offset of its binding,
where the kernel allocates an absvector of its name and offset. These are all
the kernel's Prolog functions that build a variable or read its offset, so the
rest is unchanged. Compiled Prolog code calls shen.lazyderef on every argument,
and nearly every variable is one step from its value: a primitive does that
step inline, even where shen.lazyderef is itself inlined two levels deep, as in
is!."

(defun shen.make-prolog-variable (N) (shen-jit.pvar N))

"Compiled Prolog code makes each variable it needs through shen.newpv, which calls
shen.ticket-number, shen.make-prolog-variable and shen.nextticket, and wraps what
uses it in shen.gc, which takes its offset back if that fails. Each is one inline
primitive here, which tests the vector once and makes the same stores in the same
order. Inlined code counts at its size where the lowerer decides whether to copy a
continuation into its uses, and a primitive as one call."

(defun shen.newpv (Bindings) (shen-jit.newpv Bindings shen.-null-))

(defun shen.gc (Bindings Computation) (shen-jit.gc Bindings Computation))

"A query's vector of bindings, of (prolog-memory) words, filled at each query in the
kernel. The lowerer gives it back when the query ends (shen-jit.prolog-done), and the
next query takes it, its next offset set back to 2: making a variable stores its
binding, so what an earlier query left above that offset is never read."

(defun shen.prolog-vector ()
  (let Free (trap-error (value shen-jit.*free-prolog-vectors*) (lambda E ()))
    (if (cons? Free)
        (do (set shen-jit.*free-prolog-vectors* (tl Free))
            (shen-jit.reuse-prolog-vector (hd Free) (value shen.*prolog-memory*)))
        (shen-jit.new-prolog-vector))))

"Only a vector of the size queries now take, in case (prolog-memory N) changed."

(defun shen-jit.reuse-prolog-vector (Vector Memory)
  (if (and (trap-error (do (<-address Vector (- Memory 1)) true) (lambda E false))
           (trap-error (do (<-address Vector Memory) false) (lambda E true)))
      (address-> Vector 1 2)
      (shen-jit.new-prolog-vector)))

(defun shen-jit.new-prolog-vector ()
  (let Vector (absvector (value shen.*prolog-memory*))
    (do (address-> Vector 0 shen.print-prolog-vector) (address-> Vector 1 2))))

(defun shen-jit.prolog-done (Vector Result)
  (do (set shen-jit.*free-prolog-vectors*
           (cons Vector (trap-error (value shen-jit.*free-prolog-vectors*) (lambda E ()))))
      Result))

(defun shen.pvar? (X) (shen-jit.pvar? X))

(defun shen.lazyderef (X Bindings) (shen-jit.deref X Bindings shen.-null-))

"Compiled Prolog code derefs every argument of a Shen expression, mostly variables
bound to numbers or atoms: only a pair costs a call."

(defun shen.deref (X Bindings)
  (let Value (shen.lazyderef X Bindings)
    (if (cons? Value) (shen-jit.deref-pair Value Bindings) Value)))

(defun shen-jit.deref-pair (X Bindings)
  (cons (shen.deref (hd X) Bindings) (shen.deref (tl X) Bindings)))

"(= (shen.deref X B) (shen.deref Y B)), which the Prolog compiler makes of (when (= X
Y)), compares while it dereferences, and copies neither term: the lowerer calls this
for it. It ends where they first differ, and, as shen-jit.unify= does, on a pair that
is the same object on both sides, so also on a term that holds itself, where the
copy would not end."

(defun shen-jit.deref= (X Y Bindings)
  (let A (shen.lazyderef X Bindings)
    (let B (shen.lazyderef Y Bindings)
      (if (and (cons? A) (cons? B))
          (or (shen-jit.same? A B)
              (and (shen-jit.deref= (hd A) (hd B) Bindings)
                   (shen-jit.deref= (tl A) (tl B) Bindings)))
          (= A B)))))

"Unification, as the kernel's shen.lzy= and shen.lzy=! but with no closures: it
binds what it can, keeping the variables it bound in a list, and returns that
list, or undoes them and returns false. The continuation then runs once, and a
false from it undoes them. Callers have dereferenced both terms. shen-jit.unify=!
also checks that a variable does not occur in a pair it is to be bound to. Two
lists are walked and not compared with =, which walks them too. With no occurs
check a term can hold itself, and the kernel's = ends on two such terms that are
equal: this ends on them too, as a cycle goes through a variable, and a variable
leads to one and the same object, which shen-jit.same? tells."

(defun shen-jit.undo (Trail Bindings)
  (if (cons? Trail)
      (do (shen.unwind (hd Trail) Bindings false) (shen-jit.undo (tl Trail) Bindings))
      false))

(defun shen-jit.unify= (X Y Bindings Trail)
  (cond ((and (cons? X) (cons? Y))
         (if (shen-jit.same? X Y)
             Trail
             (let A (shen.lazyderef (hd X) Bindings)
               (let D (shen.lazyderef (hd Y) Bindings)
                 (let Heads (cond ((or (cons? A) (cons? D)) (shen-jit.unify= A D Bindings Trail))
                                  ((= A D) Trail)
                                  ((shen-jit.pvar? A) (do (shen.bindv A D Bindings) (cons A Trail)))
                                  ((shen-jit.pvar? D) (do (shen.bindv D A Bindings) (cons D Trail)))
                                  (true (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings)))
                   (if (= Heads false)
                       false
                       (shen-jit.unify= (shen.lazyderef (tl X) Bindings) (shen.lazyderef (tl Y) Bindings)
                                        Bindings Heads)))))))
        ((= X Y) Trail)
        ((shen-jit.pvar? X) (do (shen.bindv X Y Bindings) (cons X Trail)))
        ((shen-jit.pvar? Y) (do (shen.bindv Y X Bindings) (cons Y Trail)))
        (true (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings))))

"The kernel's shen.occurs-check? on the term shen.deref builds, without building it."

(defun shen-jit.occurs-in? (Var Term Bindings)
  (if (cons? Term)
      (or (shen-jit.occurs-in? Var (shen.lazyderef (hd Term) Bindings) Bindings)
          (shen-jit.occurs-in? Var (shen.lazyderef (tl Term) Bindings) Bindings))
      (shen-jit.same? Var Term)))

(defun shen-jit.occurs? (Var Term Bindings)
  (and (cons? Term) (shen-jit.occurs-in? Var Term Bindings)))

"Heads that are not pairs are unified here, with no call, which is most of them."

(defun shen-jit.unify=! (X Y Bindings Trail)
  (cond ((and (cons? X) (cons? Y))
         (if (shen-jit.same? X Y)
             Trail
             (let A (shen.lazyderef (hd X) Bindings)
               (let D (shen.lazyderef (hd Y) Bindings)
                 (let Heads (cond ((or (cons? A) (cons? D)) (shen-jit.unify=! A D Bindings Trail))
                                  ((= A D) Trail)
                                  ((shen-jit.pvar? A) (do (shen.bindv A D Bindings) (cons A Trail)))
                                  ((shen-jit.pvar? D) (do (shen.bindv D A Bindings) (cons D Trail)))
                                  (true (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings)))
                   (if (= Heads false)
                       false
                       (shen-jit.unify=! (shen.lazyderef (tl X) Bindings) (shen.lazyderef (tl Y) Bindings)
                                         Bindings Heads)))))))
        ((= X Y) Trail)
        ((shen-jit.pvar? X)
         (if (shen-jit.occurs? X Y Bindings)
             (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings)
             (do (shen.bindv X Y Bindings) (cons X Trail))))
        ((shen-jit.pvar? Y)
         (if (shen-jit.occurs? Y X Bindings)
             (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings)
             (do (shen.bindv Y X Bindings) (cons Y Trail))))
        (true (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings))))

(defun shen-jit.unified (Trail Bindings C)
  (cond ((= Trail false) false)
        ((cons? Trail)
         (let Result (thaw C)
           (if (= Result false) (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings) Result)))
        (true (thaw C))))

(defun shen.lzy= (X Y Bindings C)
  (shen-jit.unified (shen-jit.unify= X Y Bindings ()) Bindings C))

(defun shen.lzy=! (X Y Bindings C)
  (shen-jit.unified (shen-jit.unify=! X Y Bindings ()) Bindings C))

(defun shen.bindv (X Value Bindings)
  (address-> Bindings (shen-jit.pvar-offset X) Value))

(defun shen.unwind (X Bindings Compute)
  (do (address-> Bindings (shen-jit.pvar-offset X) shen.-null-) Compute))

"vector? tests element 0 in one inline primitive, where the kernel's needs a
trap-error in case the absvector is empty. The kernel's shen.+vector?, behind
vector patterns, builds (vector 0) on every call to compare with; this one
builds it only when element 0 is 0. @p allocates a tuple in one step. get
looks the dictionary up itself, so that a missing entry raises once, with
the kernel's messages. Like shen.<-dict, it builds no message when the
innermost handler ignores it (shen-jit.quiet?)."

(defun vector? (X) (shen-jit.vector? X))

(defun shen.+vector? (X) (and (shen-jit.vector? X) (shen-jit.nonempty-vector? X)))

(defun shen-jit.nonempty-vector? (X) (or (not (= (<-address X 0) 0)) (not (= X (vector 0)))))

"Split so that it inlines into vector patterns: a value that is not a vector
fails there, without a call. shen.+string?, behind string patterns, tests
the type before comparing with the empty string, where the kernel's
compares first; the answer is the same for every value, and a value that
is not a string fails on one inline test."

(defun shen.+string? (X) (and (string? X) (not (= X ""))))

(defun @p (X Y) (shen-jit.absvector3 shen.tuple X Y))

(defun get (X P D)
  (let Entry (trap-error (shen-jit.dict-find D X) (lambda E ()))
    (if (cons? Entry)
        (let A (assoc P (tl Entry))
          (if (empty? A)
              (simple-error (if (shen-jit.quiet?) "" (cn "attribute " (shen.app P (cn " not found for " (shen.app X "
" shen.s)) shen.s))))
              (tl A)))
        (simple-error (if (shen-jit.quiet?) "" (shen.app X (cn " has no attributes: " (shen.app P "
" shen.s)) shen.a))))))

"Sealing. Kernel functions are
sealed: user code binds calls to them once, and redefining them raises an
error. (shen-jit.seal +) seals every definition installed from then on,
until (shen-jit.seal -); SHEN_JIT_SEAL=1 and --seal start with it on.
Stage 1 registers its arity so that Shen code can call it."

(defun shen-jit.seal (X)
  (cond ((= X +) (do (shen-jit.sealing true) +))
        ((= X -) (do (shen-jit.sealing false) -))
        (true (simple-error "shen-jit.seal: expected + or -"))))

"(shen-jit.code F) is F's bytecode, from lowering its KLambda again as
define does. Kernel functions keep no
KLambda, so ps, and this, refuse them, as in Shen/Scheme. It shows the
definition factorised when it was evaluated so (shen.record-and-evaluate
below)."

(defun shen-jit.code (F)
  (shen-jit.lower.toplevel
    (if (trap-error (get F shen-jit.factorised (value *property-vector*)) (lambda E false))
        (shen.factor (ps F))
        (ps F))
    ()))

"Byte buffers and bit operations are primitives that Shen code calls by name and
may use as values, as (fn shen-jit.u8)."

(defun shen-jit.primitive (F N) (shen-jit.set-arity F N))

"A primitive has no definition of its own, as compiled code calls it in line,
so fn's templates could not call it by name. Each gets one, made from a code
object whose body is the primitive. The special forms and eval-kl are not
primitives, and stay as they are."

(defun shen-jit.primitive-procedures ()
  (shen.for-each (lambda Group (if (number? (hd Group))
                                   (shen.for-each (lambda F (shen-jit.primitive-procedure F (hd Group)))
                                                  (hd (tl Group)))
                                   shen.skip))
                 (shen-jit.lower.fixed-names)))

(defun shen-jit.primitive-procedure (F N)
  (if (shen-jit.arity? F N)
      shen.skip
      (trap-error
        (shen-jit.install
          (cons defun (cons F (cons (cons code (cons F (cons N (cons (+ N 3) (cons ()
            (cons (cons (cons prim (cons (+ N 2) (cons F (shen-jit.slots 2 N))))
                        (cons (cons return (cons (+ N 2) ())) ()))
              (cons () ())))))))
                                  ()))))
        (lambda E shen.skip))))

(defun shen-jit.slots (From N)
  (if (= N 0) () (cons From (shen-jit.slots (+ From 1) (- N 1)))))

"An isolate starts as a program does (docs/design.md, The builder and isolates): the runtime
puts back the program's constants and makes the standard streams, then this
sets what running code reads, with the kernel's starting values: nothing of
the builder's or of the isolate that started it, whose globals are unbound
here. Its gensyms count from Base, so that names made in two isolates never
meet. The builder's own globals, macros, datatypes, the typechecker's, stay
unbound: only the builder defines."

(defun shen-jit.start-isolate (Base)
  (do (set shen-jit.*isolate* true)
  (do (set *property-vector* (shen.dict 64))
  (do (set shen.*gensym* Base)
  (do (set *hush* false)
  (do (set *maximum-print-sequence-size* 20)
  (do (set *absolute* ())
  (do (set shen.*call* 0)
  (do (set shen.*infs* 0)
  (do (set shen.*step* false)
  (do (set shen.*spy* false)
  (do (set shen.*tracking* ())
  (do (set shen.*profiled* ())
  (do (set shen.*it* "")
  (do (set shen.*residue* ())
  (do (set shen.*occurs* true)
  (do (set shen.*maxinferences* 1000000)
  (do (set shen.*names* ())
  (do (set shen.*empty-absvector* (absvector 0))
  (do (set shen.*tc* false)
  (do (set shen-jit.*free-prolog-vectors* ())
      (prolog-memory 10000))))))))))))))))))))))

"Isolates (docs/design.md, The builder and isolates): each runs a function of
one argument that the builder defined, on an OS thread of its own, and they talk
through channels. The isolate and channel namespaces are the interface; these
pass an operation's number to the primitive behind them, shen-jit.isolate
(isolate.c). A channel's receive endpoint stays with its receiver, unless
channel.give marks it to move to an isolate being spawned; send endpoints and
isolates copy freely."

(defun isolate.spawn (F X) (shen-jit.isolate 0 F X))

(defun isolate.monitor (Isolate) (shen-jit.isolate 8 Isolate ()))

(defun isolate.kill (Isolate) (shen-jit.isolate 9 Isolate ()))

(defun isolate.self () (shen-jit.isolate 10 () ()))

"What waits on something other than a channel, such as a socket, calls this now
and then, so that a kill reaches it: it raises killed if one is pending."

(defun isolate.check-kill () (shen-jit.isolate 12 () ()))

"(isolate.share V) copies V into storage outside every heap, where any isolate
reads it by pointer rather than as a copy, and nothing changes it: a store into
one of its vectors or byte buffers raises. It is never freed."

(defun isolate.share (V) (shen-jit.isolate 13 V ()))

(defun channel.new (Capacity) (shen-jit.isolate 1 Capacity ()))

(defun channel.sender (Receiver) (shen-jit.isolate 2 Receiver ()))

(defun channel.send (Sender X) (shen-jit.isolate 3 Sender X))

(defun channel.close (Endpoint) (shen-jit.isolate 6 Endpoint ()))

(defun channel.give (Receiver) (shen-jit.isolate 7 Receiver ()))

"A receive gives messages only: once a sender has closed the channel and its
queue is empty, it raises channel closed, as it does after that, the channel
being freed. The primitive gives shen-jit.end-of-stream for that end, which no
message can be. channel.next gives [ok Message] or [closed] instead, for a
stream; channel.wait-any gives [ready R] or [timeout], R having a message or
being closed."

(defun channel.receive (Receiver) (shen-jit.message (shen-jit.isolate 4 Receiver ())))

(defun channel.receive-any (Receivers)
  (let Got (shen-jit.isolate 5 Receivers ())
    (do (shen-jit.message (hd (tl Got))) Got)))

(defun shen-jit.message (X)
  (if (= X shen-jit.end-of-stream) (simple-error "channel closed") X))

(defun channel.next (Receiver)
  (let X (shen-jit.isolate 4 Receiver ())
    (if (= X shen-jit.end-of-stream) (cons closed ()) (cons ok (cons X ())))))

(defun channel.wait-any (Receivers Milliseconds)
  (let R (shen-jit.isolate 11 Receivers Milliseconds)
    (if (= R ()) (cons timeout ()) (cons ready (cons R ())))))

"A call that starts isolates for its parts and waits for them stops them if it
raises, so that none is left waiting on one that is gone, or keeping the program
from changing. (isolate.spawn-each F Args) spawns F with each of Args, each
with a monitor, and gives [[I | Monitor] ...]; if one cannot start, it stops
those it started, and raises. (isolate.stop-each Started) kills them all, as
a call's cleanup does, waits for each to end, and closes the monitors. A kill
of an isolate that has ended does nothing, and a monitor already read or closed
is passed over; a Ctrl-C ends the wait, as it ends any."

(defun isolate.spawn-each (F Args) (shen-jit.spawn-each-h F Args ()))

(defun shen-jit.spawn-each-h (F Args Started)
  (if (= () Args)
      (reverse Started)
      (let I (shen-jit.on-error (freeze (isolate.spawn F (hd Args))) (freeze (isolate.stop-each Started)))
        (let Monitor (shen-jit.on-error (freeze (isolate.monitor I))
                                        (freeze (do (isolate.kill I) (isolate.stop-each Started))))
          (shen-jit.spawn-each-h F (tl Args) (cons (cons I Monitor) Started))))))

(defun isolate.stop-each (Started)
  (do (shen-jit.kill-each Started) (shen-jit.await-each Started)))

(defun shen-jit.kill-each (Started)
  (if (= () Started)
      true
      (do (isolate.kill (hd (hd Started))) (shen-jit.kill-each (tl Started)))))

(defun shen-jit.await-each (Started)
  (if (= () Started)
      true
      (do (trap-error (do (channel.receive (tl (hd Started))) (channel.close (tl (hd Started)))) (lambda E skip))
          (shen-jit.await-each (tl Started)))))

"Types for typed programs, and the two namespaces' exports, so that a client
can write (use channel => c). An isolate is of type isolate, and endpoints of
types (receiver A) and (sender A), A being what crosses them; the shapes of
results have datatypes. The functions and the dotted types are system
functions, as the kernel's are, so that a namespace leaves them as they are
without a use. The image is built with them, from this source, which the tool
that compiles the port's Shen source could not take, as it takes definitions
only; what the datatypes define is not the program's, for shen.*userdefs*."

(defun shen-jit.isolate-interface ()
  (let Userdefs (value shen.*userdefs*)
    (do (map (lambda Form (eval Form)) (read-from-string "
(datatype isolate.types
  if (element? R [normal killed ended])
  _____________________________________
  R : isolate.reason;

  R : string;
  ___________
  R : isolate.reason;

  I : isolate; R : isolate.reason;
  ================================
  [down I R] : isolate.down;

  I : isolate; M : (receiver isolate.down);
  =========================================
  [I | M] : isolate.started;)

(datatype channel.types
  R : (receiver A);
  _________________
  R : (channel.endpoint A);

  S : (sender A);
  _______________
  S : (channel.endpoint A);

  X : A;
  ==========================
  [ok X] : (channel.next A);

  ____________________________
  [closed] : (channel.next A);

  R : (receiver A); X : A;
  ========================
  [R X] : (channel.got A);

  R : (receiver A);
  ==============================
  [ready R] : (channel.ready A);

  ______________________________
  [timeout] : (channel.ready A);)

(declare isolate.spawn [[A --> B] --> [A --> isolate]])
(declare isolate.self [--> isolate])
(declare isolate.kill [isolate --> isolate])
(declare isolate.monitor [isolate --> [receiver isolate.down]])
(declare isolate.check-kill [--> boolean])
(declare isolate.share [A --> A])
(declare isolate.spawn-each [[A --> B] --> [[list A] --> [list isolate.started]]])
(declare isolate.stop-each [[list isolate.started] --> boolean])
(declare channel.new [number --> [receiver A]])
(declare channel.sender [[receiver A] --> [sender A]])
(declare channel.give [[receiver A] --> [receiver A]])
(declare channel.send [[sender A] --> [A --> A]])
(declare channel.receive [[receiver A] --> A])
(declare channel.next [[receiver A] --> [channel.next A]])
(declare channel.receive-any [[list [receiver A]] --> [channel.got A]])
(declare channel.wait-any [[list [receiver A]] --> [number --> [channel.ready A]]])
(declare channel.close [[channel.endpoint A] --> [channel.endpoint A]])

(shen.x.namespaces.publish isolate
  [[spawn | isolate.spawn] [self | isolate.self] [kill | isolate.kill] [monitor | isolate.monitor]
   [check-kill | isolate.check-kill] [share | isolate.share] [spawn-each | isolate.spawn-each]
   [stop-each | isolate.stop-each] [down | isolate.down] [started | isolate.started]
   [reason | isolate.reason]])
(shen.x.namespaces.publish channel
  [[new | channel.new] [sender | channel.sender] [give | channel.give] [send | channel.send]
   [receive | channel.receive] [next | channel.next] [receive-any | channel.receive-any]
   [wait-any | channel.wait-any] [close | channel.close] [endpoint | channel.endpoint]
   [got | channel.got] [ready | channel.ready]])
(map (/. F (systemf F))
  [isolate.spawn isolate.self isolate.kill isolate.monitor isolate.check-kill isolate.share
   isolate.spawn-each isolate.stop-each isolate.down isolate.started isolate.reason
   channel.new channel.sender channel.give channel.send channel.receive channel.next
   channel.receive-any channel.wait-any channel.close channel.endpoint channel.got channel.ready])"))
        (set shen.*userdefs* Userdefs))))

"After each form a load evaluates. A library can do then what needs the forms
before it run, such as the grammar library compiling its loops once the
functions their guards call exist, which a file's macros cannot wait for, as
the kernel expands a whole file before it evaluates any of it.
(shen-jit.after-form F) registers F, a function of no arguments, and
(shen-jit.loading?) says whether a load is going on. These are the kernel's,
with that added. A handler around the load would keep an error in it from
recording its backtrace, so a load that raises leaves the count as it is,
and the REPL starts each input at 0."

(defun shen.load-help (Tc Forms)
  (let Depth (shen-jit.load-depth)
    (do (set shen-jit.*load-depth* (+ Depth 1))
        (let Result (if (= false Tc) (shen.eval-and-print Forms) (shen.check-eval-and-print Forms))
          (do (set shen-jit.*load-depth* Depth) Result)))))

(defun shen.eval-and-print (Forms)
  (shen.for-each (lambda Form (do (pr (shen.app (eval-kl (shen.shen->kl Form)) "
" shen.s) (stoutput)) (shen-jit.form-ended))) Forms))

(defun shen.work-through (Forms)
  (cond ((= () Forms) ())
        ((and (cons? Forms) (and (cons? (tl Forms)) (and (cons? (tl (tl Forms))) (= (hd (tl Forms)) (intern ":")))))
         (let Type (shen.typecheck (hd Forms) (hd (tl (tl Forms))))
           (if (= Type false)
               (shen.type-error)
               (let Value (eval-kl (shen.shen->kl (hd Forms)))
                 (do (pr (shen.app Value (cn " : " (shen.app (shen.pretty-type Type) "
" shen.r)) shen.s) (stoutput))
                     (do (shen-jit.form-ended) (shen.work-through (tl (tl (tl Forms))))))))))
        ((cons? Forms) (shen.work-through (cons (hd Forms) (cons (intern ":") (cons A (tl Forms))))))
        (true (simple-error "implementation error in shen.work-through"))))

(defun shen-jit.load-depth () (trap-error (value shen-jit.*load-depth*) (lambda E 0)))

(defun shen-jit.loading? () (> (shen-jit.load-depth) 0))

(defun shen-jit.after-forms () (trap-error (value shen-jit.*after-forms*) (lambda E ())))

(defun shen-jit.after-form (F) (set shen-jit.*after-forms* (adjoin F (shen-jit.after-forms))))

(defun shen-jit.form-ended () (shen-jit.run-after (reverse (shen-jit.after-forms))))

(defun shen-jit.run-after (Fs)
  (if (= Fs ()) shen.skip (do (shen-jit.apply (hd Fs) ()) (shen-jit.run-after (tl Fs)))))

"As an isolate ends, after its function returns or raises, it runs once each
function of no arguments it registered with (shen-jit.at-end F), the last first.
Then, in rounds, as collections find its guards' pointers dead, it runs each it
registered with (shen-jit.at-guards-dead F), which takes their values off the
list, as the handle library's sweep releases what its handles hold: what dies
then, while its globals still hold the rest (isolate.c).
An error from one is dropped. The builder's run only as an isolate's would:
never, as the program ends with it."

(defun shen-jit.at-end-list () (trap-error (value shen-jit.*at-end*) (lambda E ())))

(defun shen-jit.at-end (F) (set shen-jit.*at-end* (adjoin F (shen-jit.at-end-list))))

(defun shen-jit.at-guards-dead (F)
  (set shen-jit.*guards-dead* (adjoin F (trap-error (value shen-jit.*guards-dead*) (lambda E ())))))

(defun shen-jit.isolate-ending () (shen-jit.run-at-end (shen-jit.at-end-list)))

(defun shen-jit.run-at-end (Fs)
  (if (= Fs ())
      true
      (do (trap-error (shen-jit.apply (hd Fs) ()) (lambda E shen.skip)) (shen-jit.run-at-end (tl Fs)))))

"An isolate or an endpoint is an absvector whose first element is one of these,
which the printer calls."

(defun shen-jit.print-isolate (V) (cn "<isolate " (cn (str (<-address V 1)) ">")))

(defun shen-jit.print-receiver (V) (cn "<receive endpoint " (cn (str (<-address V 1)) ">")))

(defun shen-jit.print-sender (V) (cn "<send endpoint " (cn (str (<-address V 1)) ">")))

(defun shen-jit.print-given (V) (cn "<given endpoint " (cn (str (<-address V 1)) ">")))

(defun shen-jit.register-primitives ()
  (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.fixnum? 1)
  (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.tagged? 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bytes 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bytes? 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bytes-length 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.u8 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.u16 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.u32 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.u8! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.u16! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.u32! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.string->bytes 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bytes->string 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.copy-bytes 5)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bit-and 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bit-or 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bit-xor 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bit-not 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.shift-left 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.shift-right 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.rotate32 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bswap32 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.popcount 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.clz 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.ctz 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pvar 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pvar? 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pvar-offset 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.deref 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.same? 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.newpv 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.gc 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.foreign 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer? 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-kind 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-address 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bytes->pointer 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer->string 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer->bytes 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.errno 0)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.c-errno 0)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.s32 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.s64 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.f32 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.f64 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.s32! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.s64! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.f32! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.f64! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-sized 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-kill 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-guard 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-owner 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointers-dead 0)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-read-only 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.collect 0)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.write-bytes 4)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.arity 1) (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.function 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.set-arity 2) (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.apply 2) (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.isolate 3) shen.skip))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))

"Cleanup, as a library that holds something outside the heap needs it.
(shen-jit.finally Body Cleanup) thaws Body, then Cleanup however Body ended,
and gives Body's value. (shen-jit.on-error Body Cleanup) thaws Cleanup only if
Body raised, for something its caller owns once Body returns. Either way the
error raised is Body's, after Cleanup: one Cleanup raises too is added to its
message, so that it is neither lost nor put in its place."

(defun shen-jit.finally (Body Cleanup)
  (let Result (trap-error (thaw Body) (lambda E (shen-jit.failed E Cleanup)))
    (do (thaw Cleanup) Result)))

(defun shen-jit.on-error (Body Cleanup)
  (trap-error (thaw Body) (lambda E (shen-jit.failed E Cleanup))))

(defun shen-jit.failed (E Cleanup)
  (let Message (error-to-string E)
    (simple-error (trap-error (do (thaw Cleanup) Message)
                    (lambda C (cn Message (cn "; cleanup: " (error-to-string C))))))))

"Stage 1 calls this after the kernel and extensions are initialised: it
registers the port functions meant for Shen code. (shen-jit.exit N) ends
the process with status N; it is not named exit, since port definitions are
sealed and programs may define their own exit. (shen-jit.save-executable Path
Entry) writes a binary and ends the process. shen-jit.finally and
shen-jit.on-error are typed, and can be given one argument. The others are for native.shen,
which would otherwise compile its calls to them as look-ups with fn. The kernel's printers
shen.tuple, shen.pvar and shen.dictionary get arities here too: the kernel keeps only their
lambda forms, in the property vector, which an isolate starts without, so that fn makes theirs
from a template, which needs the arity."

(defun shen-jit.initialise ()
  (do (shen.initialise-arity-table
        (cons isolate.spawn (cons 2 (cons channel.new (cons 1 (cons channel.sender (cons 1
          (cons channel.send (cons 2 (cons channel.receive (cons 1 (cons channel.receive-any (cons 1
            (cons channel.close (cons 1 (cons channel.give (cons 1 (cons isolate.monitor (cons 1
              (cons isolate.kill (cons 1 (cons isolate.self (cons 0 (cons channel.next (cons 1
              (cons channel.wait-any (cons 2 (cons isolate.check-kill (cons 0 (cons isolate.share (cons 1
                (cons isolate.spawn-each (cons 2 (cons isolate.stop-each (cons 1
                (cons shen-jit.after-form (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.loading? (cons 0 (cons shen-jit.at-end (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.at-guards-dead (cons 1
                (cons shen.tuple (cons 1 (cons shen.pvar (cons 1 (cons shen.dictionary (cons 1
                (cons shen-jit.print-isolate (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.print-receiver (cons 1
                  (cons shen-jit.print-sender (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.print-given (cons 1
                    ())))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
  (do (eval-kl (shen-jit.template-defun shen-jit.template-2 (cons a (cons b ()))))
  (do (eval-kl (shen-jit.template-defun shen-jit.template-3 (cons a (cons b (cons c ())))))
  (do (eval-kl (shen-jit.template-defun shen-jit.template-4 (cons a (cons b (cons c (cons d ()))))))
  (do (shen-jit.struct.initialise)
  (do (shen-jit.isolate-interface)
  (do (shen-jit.register-primitives)
  (do (shen-jit.primitive-procedures)
  (do (shen.initialise-arity-table
        (cons shen-jit.seal (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.backtrace (cons 0
          (cons shen-jit.exit (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.code (cons 1
            (cons shen-jit.save-executable (cons 2
              (cons shen-jit.install (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.symbol? (cons 1
                (cons shen-jit.sealed? (cons 1
                  (cons shen-jit.lower.builder (cons 3 (cons shen-jit.lower.emit (cons 2
                    (cons shen-jit.lower.bind (cons 2 (cons shen-jit.lower.reserve (cons 2
                      (cons shen-jit.lower.constant (cons 2 (cons shen-jit.lower.add-code (cons 2
                        (cons shen-jit.lower.code-form (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.lower.prim-arity (cons 1
                          (cons shen-jit.lower.fixed? (cons 1
                            (cons shen-jit.finally (cons 2 (cons shen-jit.on-error (cons 2 ())))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
      (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (shen.lambda-entry shen-jit.finally))
      (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (shen.lambda-entry shen-jit.on-error))
      (do (declare shen-jit.fixnum? (cons A (cons --> (cons boolean ()))))
      (do (declare shen-jit.finally (cons (cons lazy (cons A ()))
                                      (cons --> (cons (cons (cons lazy (cons B ())) (cons --> (cons A ()))) ()))))
      (do (declare shen-jit.on-error (cons (cons lazy (cons A ()))
                                       (cons --> (cons (cons (cons lazy (cons B ())) (cons --> (cons A ()))) ()))))
      (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (shen.lambda-entry shen-jit.seal))
          (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (shen.lambda-entry shen-jit.exit))
              (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (shen.lambda-entry shen-jit.code))
                  (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (shen.lambda-entry shen-jit.save-executable))
                      (set shen-jit.defined ()))))))))))))))))))))

"Definitions are factorised as they are evaluated, as Shen/Scheme's compiler
does: the kernel's shen.factor binds each selector once and shares the tests
of clauses that start alike, instead of recomputing (tl (tl ... X)) in every
test of every clause. Only what define produced is factorised, since
shen.factor expects its shape, and what is recorded for ps stays as define
produced it; shen.*factorise?* stays false, as in Shen/Scheme. The kernel's
shen.shendef->kldef-h, with the definition remembered:"

(defun shen.shendef->kldef-h (F Rules)
  (let Patterns (map (lambda R (fst R)) Rules)
    (let Arity (shen.arity-chk F Patterns)
      (let Checked (map (lambda R (shen.free-var-chk F R)) Rules)
        (let Kl (shen.factorise-code (shen.compile-to-kl F (shen.unprotect Rules) Arity))
          (do (set shen-jit.defined Kl) Kl))))))

"The kernel's shen.record-and-evaluate, evaluating that definition factorised:"

(defun shen.record-and-evaluate (Kl)
  (if (and (cons? Kl) (and (= defun (hd Kl)) (and (cons? (tl Kl)) (and (cons? (tl (tl Kl)))
        (and (cons? (tl (tl (tl Kl)))) (= () (tl (tl (tl (tl Kl))))))))))
      (let F (hd (tl Kl))
        (do (if (shen.sysfunc? F)
                (simple-error (shen.app F " is not a legitimate function name
" shen.a))
                shen.skip)
            (do (shen.store-arity F (length (hd (tl (tl Kl)))))
                (do (shen.record-kl F Kl)
                    (let Factor (= Kl (value shen-jit.defined))
                      (do (put F shen-jit.factorised Factor (value *property-vector*))
                          (do (eval-kl (if Factor (shen.factor Kl) Kl))
                              (shen.fn-print F))))))))
      Kl))

"absvector fills a new vector with (fail) already, so vector only sets the
length stamp; the kernel's version fills it again."

(defun vector (N) (address-> (absvector (+ N 1)) 0 N))

"<-vector is the kernel's, split in two so that each part is small enough
to inline: a failing <-address,
or either error, then happens in the caller's frame, where a quiet handler
catches it with a jump. (fail) is the symbol shen.fail!."

(defun <-vector (V N)
  (if (= N 0)
      (simple-error "cannot access 0th element of a vector
")
      (shen-jit.vector-element (<-address V N))))

"integer? is a primitive, as in Shen/Scheme: the kernel's finds a power of
two above the number and subtracts its way down, which took 21 ns for 7 and
343 ns for 123456789. A fixnum is an integer; so is a finite real without a
fraction."

(defun integer? (X) (shen-jit.integer? X))

"append copies its first list in C, in one block: the kernel's recursion
builds one pair per call on its way back. The empty list stays inline. A
list that does not end in [] raises the kernel's error."

(defun append (X Y) (if (= X ()) Y (shen-jit.append X Y)))

"The reader asks shen.misc? about every byte of a symbol that is not a
lowercase letter, digits included; the kernel's is an element? over a list
of 19 codes. The same codes as ranges: 33, 35-39, 42-43, 45-47, 60-64,
95-96 and 126. Anything but a number is not one, as with element?."

(defun shen.misc? (X)
  (and (number? X)
       (or (= X 33) (or (and (>= X 35) (<= X 39)) (or (and (>= X 42) (<= X 43))
         (or (and (>= X 45) (<= X 47)) (or (and (>= X 60) (<= X 64))
           (or (and (>= X 95) (<= X 96)) (= X 126)))))))))

"The reader's rules call shen.<-out for every output and shen.hds=? for
every one-byte rule, and the kernel's are too large to inline. The kernel's
shen.<-out tests the shape of its argument and then takes (hd (tl X)) either
way, so the test changes nothing."

(defun shen.<-out (X) (hd (tl X)))

(defun shen.hds=? (X B) (and (cons? X) (= (hd X) B)))

"Runs of bytes, which the kernel's rules read one rule call per byte, with a
parse result for each: a comment up to a return byte (9, 10 or 13, as
shen.return?), whitespace, and digits, which every token is first tried as.
Each gives what the kernel's gives."

(defun shen.<shortnatters> (Bytes) (shen.comb (shen-jit.to-return Bytes) shen.skip))

(defun shen-jit.to-return (Bytes)
  (if (cons? Bytes)
      (let B (hd Bytes)
        (if (or (= B 10) (or (= B 13) (= B 9)))
            Bytes
            (shen-jit.to-return (tl Bytes))))
      Bytes))

(defun shen.<whitespaces> (Bytes)
  (if (and (cons? Bytes) (shen.whitespace? (hd Bytes)))
      (shen.comb (shen-jit.skip-whitespace (tl Bytes)) shen.skip)
      (shen.parse-failure)))

(defun shen-jit.skip-whitespace (Bytes)
  (if (cons? Bytes)
      (let B (hd Bytes)
        (if (or (= B 32) (or (= B 10) (or (= B 13) (= B 9))))
            (shen-jit.skip-whitespace (tl Bytes))
            Bytes))
      Bytes))

(defun shen.<digits> (Bytes)
  (if (and (cons? Bytes) (shen.digit? (hd Bytes)))
      (shen-jit.digits Bytes)
      (shen.parse-failure)))

(defun shen-jit.digits (Bytes)
  (let Rest (tl Bytes)
    (if (and (cons? Rest) (shen.digit? (hd Rest)))
        (let Digits (shen-jit.digits Rest)
          (shen.comb (hd Digits) (cons (shen.byte->digit (hd Bytes)) (hd (tl Digits)))))
        (shen.comb Rest (cons (shen.byte->digit (hd Bytes)) ())))))

"A symbol's characters, joined with the same cn and n->string as the
kernel's shen.<alphanum> and shen.<alphanums>, without their rule calls."

(defun shen.<alphanums> (Bytes)
  (if (and (cons? Bytes) (or (shen.alpha? (hd Bytes)) (shen.digit? (hd Bytes))))
      (let Rest (shen.<alphanums> (tl Bytes))
        (shen.comb (hd Rest) (cn (n->string (hd Bytes)) (hd (tl Rest)))))
      (shen.comb Bytes "")))

"The reader's string-to-byte-list conversion reads one buffer backwards,
instead of copying the remaining string with tlstr at each byte."

(defun shen.str->bytes (S)
  (if (string? S)
      (let Buffer (shen-jit.string->bytes S)
        (shen-jit.buffer->list Buffer (- (shen-jit.bytes-length Buffer) 1) ()))
      (shen.f-error shen.str->bytes)))

(defun shen-jit.buffer->list (Buffer I Acc)
  (if (< I 0)
      Acc
      (shen-jit.buffer->list Buffer (- I 1) (cons (shen-jit.u8 Buffer I) Acc))))

"An atom is a string, a number or a symbol, tried in that order. Every
alternative of shen.<number> starts with a digit or one of - + . (bytes 45,
43 and 46), so the kernel's number rules, five calls deep, are tried only
on those; for the rest they fail anyway."

(defun shen.<atom> (Bytes)
  (let Str (shen.<str> Bytes)
    (if (shen.parse-failure? Str)
        (let Number (if (shen-jit.number-start? Bytes) (shen.<number> Bytes) (shen.parse-failure))
          (if (shen.parse-failure? Number)
              (let Sym (shen.<sym> Bytes)
                (if (shen.parse-failure? Sym)
                    Sym
                    (let S (shen.<-out Sym)
                      (shen.comb (shen.in-> Sym) (if (= S "<>") (cons vector (cons 0 ())) (intern S))))))
              Number))
        Str)))

(defun shen-jit.number-start? (Bytes)
  (and (cons? Bytes)
       (let B (hd Bytes) (or (shen.digit? B) (or (= B 45) (or (= B 43) (= B 46)))))))

(defun shen-jit.vector-element (X)
  (if (= X shen.fail!)
      (simple-error "vector element not found
")
      X))

"read-file, behind load and script, skips a first line that starts with #!,
so that a script can run directly. The rest
is the kernel's definition."

(defun read-file (File)
  (let Bytes (shen-jit.skip-shebang (read-file-as-bytelist File))
    (let Sexprs (trap-error (compile (lambda X (shen.<s-exprs> X)) Bytes)
                            (lambda E (shen.reader-error (value shen.*residue*))))
      (shen.process-sexprs Sexprs))))

"read-file-as-bytelist and read-file-as-string read the whole file in C, as
Shen/Scheme's do; the kernel's read one read-byte at a time, and
read-file-as-string joins the bytes with one cn each. Paths are relative to
*home-directory*, as for open."

(defun read-file-as-bytelist (File) (shen-jit.read-file File true))

(defun read-file-as-string (File) (shen-jit.read-file File false))

"pr writes a string to a byte stream at once: its bytes copied to a buffer,
then one shen-jit.write-bytes, where the kernel's takes a write-byte and a pos
under a handler for each byte, and finds the end by the error the last raises.
As there, a value that is not a string, or the empty string, writes nothing,
to any stream."

(defun pr (S Stream)
  (cond ((value *hush*) S)
        ((char-stream? Stream) (write-string S Stream))
        ((and (string? S) (not (= S "")))
         (let Bytes (shen-jit.string->bytes S)
           (do (shen-jit.write-bytes Bytes 0 (shen-jit.bytes-length Bytes) Stream) S)))
        (true S)))

"shen.record-kl, behind every definition, adds the name to shen.*userdefs*
with adjoin, which looks through the whole list, so loading n new
definitions took time growing with n squared. A name without a shen.source
property has never been recorded, so it goes on the list directly; any other
still goes through adjoin."

(defun shen.record-kl (F Kl)
  (do (set shen.*userdefs* (if (shen-jit.recorded? F)
                               (adjoin F (value shen.*userdefs*))
                               (cons F (value shen.*userdefs*))))
      (put F shen.source Kl (value *property-vector*))))

(defun shen-jit.recorded? (F)
  (let Entry (shen-jit.dict-find (value *property-vector*) F)
    (and (cons? Entry) (cons? (assoc shen.source (tl Entry))))))

(defun shen-jit.skip-shebang (Bytes)
  (if (and (cons? Bytes) (and (= (hd Bytes) 35) (and (cons? (tl Bytes)) (= (hd (tl Bytes)) 33))))
      (shen-jit.skip-line Bytes)
      Bytes))

(defun shen-jit.skip-line (Bytes)
  (cond ((= () Bytes) ())
        ((= (hd Bytes) 10) Bytes)
        (true (shen-jit.skip-line (tl Bytes)))))

"symbol? and variable? by the kernel's rules for names, in C. The kernel
tests the characters of (str X), which builds a string and scans it, and
expects str to fail on values other than atoms; shen-jit's str describes
them instead, and # < > are symbol characters, so #<procedure> would pass."

(defun symbol? (X) (> (shen-jit.symbol-kind X) 0))

(defun variable? (X) (= (shen-jit.symbol-kind X) 2))

"The reader's number overrides (docs/semantics.md, section 3). In Shen/Scheme a
zero digit adds an exact 0 to a fraction, so 8.0 reads as the integer 8;
skipping zero digits gives the same values here. Literals beyond the fixnum
range become the nearest real instead of overflowing."

(defun shen.compute-fraction-h (Digits Expt)
  (cond ((= () Digits) 0)
        ((= 0 (hd Digits)) (shen.compute-fraction-h (tl Digits) (- Expt 1)))
        (true (+ (* (shen.expt 10 Expt) (hd Digits))
                 (shen.compute-fraction-h (tl Digits) (- Expt 1))))))

(defun shen.compute-integer (Digits)
  (trap-error (shen-jit.horner Digits 0)
              (lambda E (shen-jit.decimal (shen-jit.digits-text Digits "") 0))))

(defun shen-jit.horner (Digits N)
  (if (= () Digits) N (shen-jit.horner (tl Digits) (+ (* N 10) (hd Digits)))))

(defun shen-jit.digits-text (Digits Text)
  (if (= () Digits) Text (shen-jit.digits-text (tl Digits) (cn Text (str (hd Digits))))))

(defun shen.compute-E (N Log10)
  (trap-error (* N (shen.expt 10 Log10)) (lambda E (shen-jit.decimal (str N) Log10))))
